 1 big thing: Dems shatter GOP statehouse control 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Axios research; Map: Andrew Pantazi/Axios Democrats are on track to control most state legislative chambers, ending 16 years of Republican dominance over America's statehouses, Axios' Andrew Pantazi reports. 

 Tuesday's midterm results have dramatically expanded Democrats' power to increase minimum wages, protect abortion access and set voting rules ahead of 2028. 😳 Democrats are set to flip 12 legislative chambers, dismantle five Republican state trifectas (both statehouse chambers plus governorship), and shatter 11 GOP supermajorities, per AP .

 If current leads hold, 75% of Americans will live in a state with a Democratic governor, and 53% will live in Democratic trifectas. As it stands, Democrats will control the governor's office and both chambers in five of the seven 2024 presidential battlegrounds: Arizona, Michigan, Nevada, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin.

 They have also broken Republican one-party rule in the sixth (Georgia) and flipped the House in the seventh (North Carolina). 🫏 Democrats flipped five GOP-held governorships (Georgia, Iowa, Nevada, Ohio and Texas) and lead in Alaska.

 Republicans haven't flipped any, despite targeting Arizona, Michigan, Oregon and Wisconsin. Kansas, where Democrat Cindy Holscher is leading by about half a point, remains uncalled. The bottom line: Republicans spent 16 years building a statehouse majority. Democrats erased it in one night — and they now have two years to show voters what they'll do with it.

